//? Title: ResNet Basic Blocks: Shortcuts, Post-Add Gates and Complete Depth
//? Subtitle: Tensor computations, explicit checks and reproducible local figures
//? Exports: both
//? Lang: en-GB

\abstract(
Adding layers can make fitting harder even when a larger model could represent the desired
mapping. Residual blocks add learned corrections to a shortcut path. We check the exact
post-activation rule and dimension-changing projection, then train complete compact ResNet-18
and ResNet-34 models with visible tensor Adam loops and separate batch-normalisation state.
)
\toc

Source example: `examples/cpp/source/nn/1_convolutional_neural_networks/1.3_residual_and_dense_families/resnet_basic.cpp`.

> The method

A basic block has two bias-free 3×3 convolutions. The first is followed by BN/ReLU; the
second has BN without ReLU before addition. The shortcut is an identity only when channels
and stride match; otherwise it is a bias-free 1×1 projection with BN. The block is

$$
y=\operatorname{ReLU}(F(x)+S(x)),\qquad
J_y=\operatorname{diag}(F(x)+S(x)>0)(J_F+J_S).
$$
\alttext(An output ReLU gates the sum of residual and shortcut derivatives.)

Even if F=0 and S is identity, the result is ReLU(x), which clips negative inputs and their
derivatives. The toy sets the final branch BN gamma to zero so the residual is exactly zero.
Its projection variant downsamples a 5×5×2 input to 3×3 and copies two channels into four.
Projection BN gamma sqrt(1+epsilon) cancels inference variance one plus epsilon. A tensor
slice-and-duplicate reference checks values and input derivatives, including stride alignment.

ResNet-18 uses block counts 2/2/2/2; ResNet-34 uses 3/4/6/3. Learned depth is one stem
convolution plus two per block plus one classifier; shortcut projections are not counted in
that name. Reference stage widths are 64/128/256/512. The standard stem uses a 7×7 stride-two
convolution with symmetric pad three, then 3×3 stride-two max-pooling with negative-infinity
pad one. At 224 pixels, stem and stage sides are 56, then 56/28/14/7. These explicit edge
conventions are not a bitwise Caffe compatibility claim. GAP feeds a linear 1000-class head.
Learned/moving scalar counts are 11689512/9600 and 21797672/17024, respectively.

At constant width C, two 3×3 branch kernels cost 18 C² weights and O(NHW 18 C²) MACs,
plus BN and any projection. BN learns gamma/beta with epsilon 1e-5; its moving mean and
unbiased EMA variance live in a separate store. No dropout or pretrained weights are used.
Full forward geometry/counts are checked without backward storage. All parameter gradients
are checked separately at one-eighth widths on a two-image training batch, retaining depth.

The dataset fits use quarter widths and a 3×3 stride-one stem without pooling on original
32×32 CIFAR-10 images. This four-stage adaptation is not the paper's three-stage CIFAR ResNet.
Each complete depth runs 6 epochs, batch eight, Adam(.001), explicit kernel L2=.0001/2 and
independent padded crops/flips. The split is 128 fit/64 validation/64 official-test rows.
Validation selects and restores both parameters and BN moving moments before test evaluation.
Cached CIFAR-10 is required; these are bounded workflow demonstrations.

The editable concept diagram is `diagrams/resnet_basic_concept.tikz`. Regeneration uses
LaTeX with PGF/TikZ and standalone, plus dvisvgm; exports use the saved SVG.

```{bash, results="output", file="figures/resnet_basic_concept.svg"}
set -eu
mkdir -p build
latex -interaction=batchmode -halt-on-error -output-directory=build diagrams/resnet_basic_concept.tikz > build/resnet_basic_concept.compile.log 2>&1
dvisvgm --no-fonts --bbox=10 --output=build/resnet_basic_concept.svg build/resnet_basic_concept.dvi > build/resnet_basic_concept.render.log 2>&1
printf 'Rendered local concept diagram.\n'
```
